    "en": {
      "category": "Health and prevention",
      "subcategory": "Lifestyle diseases & medication",
      "title": "What diet guidance actually looks like for lifestyle-related conditions",
      "excerpt": "“Am I about to be told everything is off-limits?” — that's the look I see on so many patients' faces after a checkup. Here's why I don't hand out strict diet rules, and the one thing I ask instead.",
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "\"Your triglycerides are a bit high,\" \"your blood sugar is something to watch,\" \"your blood pressure is on the high side\" — throughout my career, I've often watched a patient's face fall the moment I said something like this. When I asked, many told me they'd already braced themselves for a long list of forbidden foods, or for being told to count calories and track every gram of salt. I understand why that feeling makes people put off coming in at all."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "By the end of this post, you'll see that detailed food restrictions are rarely necessary at all — and you'll have one simple idea you can start today instead. The short version: the one piece of advice I give patients managing a lifestyle-related condition is simply **\"try not to eat the same thing every day.\"** Here's why that's enough."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "In practice, throughout my career, I've rarely handed out strict rules like \"never eat this\" or \"this is completely off-limits.\""
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### The stricter the rule, the harder it is to keep"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "I've seen it again and again: the more conscientious a patient is, the more they struggle under strict instructions. \"No more than this many grams of rice per meal.\" \"No fried food, ever.\" \"No eating out.\" Rules that specific turn planning each day's meals into a burden, and sooner or later, people either give up entirely or beat themselves up for slipping. Either way, it moves further from the actual goal."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Eating is something you do for the rest of your life. Numbers that drop after a few weeks of gritting your teeth mean little if you simply go back to how you ate before. **What matters is how long you can actually keep something up.** That's why I try to offer something light enough to start today, rather than a set of rules that stops you in your tracks."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### The only thing I ask: eat something different each day"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "The one piece of advice I give most often to patients managing a lifestyle-related condition is simply this: **try not to eat the same thing every day.**"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "People are sometimes surprised that's all there is to it, but keeping that one idea in mind tends to change things more than you'd expect. Stepping away from fixed patterns — banana and toast every morning, the same bowl of ramen every lunch — and choosing something different each day naturally makes it harder to overdo any one nutrient, whether that's fat, carbohydrates, or salt. In my experience, eating a variety of real food day to day does more for a balanced intake of vitamins, minerals, and fiber than reaching for several kinds of supplements."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### No need to change the cooking — just the ingredients and the colors"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Thinking \"I have to cook something completely different every day\" would be its own burden. All I'm really asking is that you vary **the ingredients and the colors on the table.**"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "For a main dish, that might mean rotating pork on Monday, a fish rich in omega-3s on Tuesday, tofu on Wednesday. For vegetables, it might mean adding a color you didn't have yesterday — green (spinach, broccoli), red (tomato, carrot), yellow (pumpkin, egg), black (hijiki seaweed, mushrooms). That's enough. Something like, \"I had fish yesterday, so today let's do chicken and natto,\" or \"yesterday was mostly green, so let's add a red tomato today\" — treating it almost like a small game is about the right amount of effort to keep it up."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### A message from the director"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Of course, for patients who already have reduced kidney function, or whose numbers are quite high, I do sometimes ask for stricter nutritional adjustments for a period. But for most lifestyle-related conditions, what actually matters isn't perfection — it's **keeping up a few small, good habits for a long time.**"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "If your checkup results weren't great and you're not sure where to start, or if you've been taking supplements but aren't sure they're doing anything, please feel free to come talk it over with us. I won't hand you a list of strict rules. I'd rather work out, together, the smallest step that actually fits your life."
        }
      ]
    }
