    "en": {
      "category": "Health and prevention",
      "subcategory": "Infections & vaccines",
      "title": "Still unsure about the flu shot? Here's how to think about it",
      "excerpt": "I hear people get the flu even after the shot, and the side effects worry me too — if that loop sounds familiar, here's the real reason I've always told patients the choice is ultimately theirs.",
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "\"Should I get the flu shot?\" It's a question I've heard constantly, every year around this time, throughout my career. \"I hear people get it even after getting vaccinated...\" \"The side effects worry me.\" \"If you could just tell me it's completely safe and definitely works, I'd get it.\" And so the hesitation drags on, and another year slips by without a decision. I understand that feeling completely."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "By the end of this post, you'll be able to choose — not because someone told you to, but because **you've reached your own conclusion.**"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### What \"it's really up to you\" actually means"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "When I say this, I'm not brushing you off. A vaccine is different in nature from treatment given after you're already sick. With treatment, there's usually little real room for hesitation. A vaccine, though, is about **choosing, for yourself, how to protect your body against a risk that hasn't happened yet** — something that, by its nature, belongs to you."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Honestly, as a doctor, I can't tell you \"this vaccine guarantees complete safety\" or \"you definitely won't get the flu.\" The flu shot lowers your risk of getting sick and of severe illness, but it doesn't eliminate it entirely. That \"not 100%\" is exactly why the final call belongs to you."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "That said, there's information worth knowing as you weigh it. Older adults and young children, people managing a chronic condition, and anyone living with young children or elderly family members tend to see a bigger benefit from vaccination, both in avoiding severe illness and in protecting the people around them. On the other hand, if you've had a serious allergic reaction in the past, that calls for a more careful conversation. None of this is meant to hand you an answer — it's material for your own decision. You're still the one holding the wheel."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### A message from the director"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Saying \"it's your decision\" can sometimes come across as cold, as if I'm pushing the question back onto you. I'll admit, that worry sits with me every time I say it."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Even so, I believe that **letting you hold the wheel on your own health is the most honest form of medicine I can offer.** Our role as physicians isn't to give orders. I want to hand you clear, accurate information about the benefits and the risks, and then stand beside you as a **partner** in whatever you decide."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Hesitation and all, please feel free to bring it up with me in the exam room. Let's think it through together."
        }
      ]
    }
