    "en": {
      "category": "Health and prevention",
      "subcategory": "Infections & vaccines",
      "title": "FluMist, the nasal-spray flu vaccine — is it okay with a runny nose?",
      "excerpt": "“I heard there's a spray vaccine for kids who hate needles” — I've been hearing this more and more. What many people don't know is that how well it works can depend on the condition of your nose.",
      "body": [
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "\"I heard there's a flu vaccine you spray in the nose instead of a shot... can my child have it?\" In my practice, I've been hearing this question more and more around this time of year. It's FluMist, the nasal-spray flu vaccine that became available in Japan from 2024. For children who hate needles — and for parents who face tears every year — it's a genuinely reassuring option."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "By the end of this post, you'll understand how FluMist works and **what to check before getting it.** The short answer: **because FluMist works directly on the lining of the nose, it may not work as well when there's a lot of nasal discharge or congestion.** If you're going to get it, it's worth making sure the nose is in good condition first."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### Why go through the nose?"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "A standard flu shot (an inactivated vaccine) is injected into the arm and prepares the body by building antibodies in the blood. FluMist, on the other hand, is a **live vaccine** made from a weakened virus, and it's sprayed directly onto the **lining of the nose** — the very entry point where the flu virus actually gets in."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "This builds immunity (IgA antibodies) right at the virus's \"front door,\" so the virus can be stopped as it tries to enter. For this to work, though, **the sprayed liquid needs to make good contact with the nasal lining and stay there.**"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### Why a runny or stuffy nose can reduce its effectiveness"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Take particular care in situations like these:"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "- Heavy discharge or congestion from allergic rhinitis or hay fever\n- A runny nose from a cold\n- Irritated nasal lining from blowing the nose too much"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "With a lot of discharge, the sprayed vaccine can simply run back out before it settles on the lining. And when the lining is swollen from inflammation, the vaccine may not attach properly. So that you don't end up with \"a painless vaccine that didn't fully work,\" **checking the condition of the nose beforehand** is very important."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### What to check before the appointment"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "If your child has allergic rhinitis, treating it beforehand to calm the symptoms helps FluMist work as intended."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "If a runny or stuffy nose is severe on the day, don't push it — **postponing** until symptoms settle, or **switching to the injectable vaccine,** are both options. Especially when you're unsure (\"there's a bit of a runny nose — is that okay?\"), please don't hesitate to tell the doctor. After checking inside the nose, we can decide together whether that day is right for the vaccine."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Also, because FluMist uses a live virus, **it's generally for people aged 2 to under 19.** Even within that age range, it may not be recommended for people who:"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "- Have asthma, or a history of recurrent wheezing\n- Have a severe egg allergy\n- Have a condition that weakens the immune system, or are on immunosuppressive treatment\n- Are pregnant"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "If you have any medical conditions or allergies, **please be sure to tell the doctor beforehand.**"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "### A message from the director"
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "Having a painless spray vaccine as an option is genuinely good news, for children and parents alike."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "That said, rather than choosing it only because \"it doesn't hurt,\" **choosing it with your child's current condition in mind** is what ultimately protects them best."
        },
        {
          "kind": "text",
          "text": "If you're wondering \"Is my child's nose okay for it right now?\" or \"Should we go with the shot or the spray?\", please feel free to ask. Let's think it through together."
        }
      ]
    }
